package factory

// StageMethod leaves a person's replacement ask free of shipped instructions.
func StageMethod(st Stage) string {
	shipped, ok := ShippedStage(st.Name)
	if !ok || st.Ask != shipped.Ask {
		return ""
	}
	return stageMethods[st.Name]
}

var stageMethods = map[string]string{
	"plan":     "Understand the issue before deciding: follow the code it reaches, its tests and conventions. Change no files. Write the problem and observable acceptance; assumptions and what would disprove each; the smallest end-to-end slices in order, each leaving working behaviour; each slice's public test interface and expected behaviour; risks and what stays out of scope. Give each planned file a reason. Finish when someone new to the issue could implement the plan. If competing readings require different designs and the code cannot settle them, ask; otherwise choose the simpler reading and name the assumption.",
	"write":    "Implement the plan, one slice at a time. Write a test through a public interface, run it and observe the expected failure; write the least code that passes, then rerun it. For a bug, that failure is the reproduction: fix the cause where it lives. If behaviour cannot be reached by a test, explain the gap and check it directly where possible. Keep unrelated discoveries in notes. Make and report small plan corrections; ask before changing the design. Finish when every slice is implemented and its tests pass on the latest code; otherwise report what remains incomplete.",
	"test":     "Find the repository's test commands in its own files. Run checks covering the change, then the wider behaviour it could break; read their exit status and failures. Diagnose each failure before editing: fix wrong code, or correct a wrong test with the reason, preserving its intended contract. Attribute an older failure only with supporting history or recorded runs; keep the checkout intact. Finish after the relevant checks pass on the latest code, or report done false with failures, unavailable checks and why. Ask when the expected behaviour cannot be resolved from the issue and code.",
	"review":   "Review and repair the diff. Read the diff, callers, error paths and tests. Judge separately: does it satisfy the issue and plan within scope; does it handle edges and failures and follow written repository standards? Each finding needs a failing path or broken standard, file, line and severity: blocker, major or minor. Taste belongs outside findings. Fix blockers and majors, add a regression test for changed behaviour and run it. Put minors in notes. Report findings and their disposition; count only unresolved blockers and majors. Finish with none open; otherwise report incomplete. Name what you read and the riskiest path checked, including a clean review. Ask if resolving a finding requires a product decision.",
	"security": "Trace untrusted input from entry to use; check authorization for changed actions and secrets reaching code, logs or errors. A finding needs a reachable attack path, its effect, file, line and severity. Put unsupported concerns in notes. Repair reachable vulnerabilities, adding and running a regression test where possible. Finish when no demonstrated vulnerability remains; otherwise report incomplete with open findings. Say what you traced, what you could not check and why. Ask when the intended trust boundary or permission is unclear.",
	"proof":    "Challenge every issue promise and earlier claim: choose an observation that could disprove it, run the relevant test or use the program as a person would, and read the result. Use fresh command receipts from this round on the current checkout; inspection alone cannot prove tested behaviour. Run only what the claims need. Report each claim with what you observed. Leave an unverified claim ok false with the reason. Finish only when all claims are shown; otherwise report incomplete. Ask when acceptance is ambiguous, rather than choosing an easier claim.",
	"read":     "Inspect without changing files. Explain what the change does and whether it matches its description and linked issue. Trace the highest-risk paths through callers and tests. Report risks worst first with file and reason, open questions, and what you did not read. Finish when the description and risks are grounded in the code; report incomplete if missing material prevents that assessment. Ask when missing intent changes the assessment.",
	"checks":   "For each claim choose what would disprove it, find the repository's way to check it, run it and read the exit and output. Report one claim per check with the actual command and result. If no check can reach a claim, leave it ok false and explain why. Finish when each claim has an observed result; report incomplete for failed or unavailable checks. Ask when a claim has no clear acceptance criterion.",
	"bisect":   "Reproduce with one fast command and observe it fail for the reported reason. Confirm a passing and failing revision with that command, then narrow between them if revision movement is permitted. Change no code and leave the checkout on its branch. If movement is refused, narrow using history and recorded runs; distinguish those records from runs you performed and state which revisions you did not run. Report the first failing change only when supported, results either side and the causal link. Otherwise report done false with the narrowed range and attempts. Ask if a missing reproduction or revision prevents further narrowing.",
	"fix":      "Run the reproduction and observe the reported failure before editing. Find its cause, using the bisect result if available. Fix it where it originates with the least necessary change; add a regression test that fails without the fix if none exists. Run the reproduction to green, then surrounding tests on the latest code. Weakening a check, adding retries or widening a limit requires evidence that the check, retry policy or limit caused the defect. Finish only when the reproduction and relevant tests pass; otherwise report incomplete. Ask when the expected behaviour is unresolved.",
}
